---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Booking software for community studios"
	description="Booking software for community and membership pottery studios: wheel time reservations, shared kiln firing queues, open-studio check-in and classes in one place."
>
	<section class="band-dark page-hero">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">For community studios</p>
				<h1>One studio, a hundred members, three kilns.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Community studios run on shared everything: shared wheels, shared shelves, shared kilns.
					Kilnbook keeps track of who booked what, whose work is in which load, and who is in the
					building during open-studio hours.
				</p>
				<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
			</div>
			<img
				src="/images/studio-floor.svg"
				alt="A community studio floor with rows of wheels, hand-building tables and kilns along the back wall"
				width="480"
				height="309"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Sound familiar?</p>
			<h2>What gets hard as a membership grows</h2>
			<div class="cards">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>The wheel scramble</h3>
					<p>
						Every Tuesday evening, the same members get the good wheels and everyone else waits.
						A sign-up sheet on the door doesn't stop anyone from staying an extra two hours.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>The kiln shelf mystery</h3>
					<p>
						Unlabeled pots on the bisque shelf, firing fees counted by hand at the end of the month,
						and finished pieces nobody has picked up since spring.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Members and students on one calendar</h3>
					<p>
						Classes need the wheels too. Without one schedule, a Wheel 101 course and an open-studio
						session end up booked into the same room on the same night.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Wheel time that's fair to every tier</h2>
		<p>
			Members reserve wheels, hand-building tables and shared equipment in one-hour blocks. You set
			how many hours each membership tier gets a week and how far ahead they can book, and make
			evenings and weekends count double if that's when the studio is full. A tablet by the door
			handles open-studio check-in, so you always know who is in the building.
			<a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel time works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Shared kilns without the shelf chaos</h2>
		<p>
			Members log each piece from their phone when they put it on the shelf and pay the firing fee
			by cubic inch right there. Every kiln gets its own bisque and glaze queues, and members get a
			text with a photo when their load comes out.
			<a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Classes that share the studio</h2>
		<p>
			Run beginner courses, drop-ins and class packs alongside memberships. Waitlists fill cancelled
			seats on their own, and plenty of studios use classes as the way new members find them.
			<a href="/features/classes">Classes and packs</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Which plan fits a community studio?</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios start on the Studio plan at $79 a month. It covers up to 3 kilns with
			unlimited members, wheel and table reservations, class packs, waitlists, memberships and
			pickup texts. If you add a fourth kiln or a second location, Collective has no kiln limit.
			<a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Give your members a studio that runs itself.</h2>
			<p>30 days free on the Studio plan. No card to start, and nothing is deleted if you walk away.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
